{:edb/db-before-t 1573 :edb/db-after-t 1574 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534886 50 #inst "2026-10-08T10:08:52.758Z" 13194139534886 true] [17592186155315 1104 #edb/ref 17592186155320 13194139534886 true] [17592186155324 1104 #edb/ref 17592186155327 13194139534886 true] [17592186155330 1104 #edb/ref 17592186155333 13194139534886 true] [17592186157384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317453 13194139534886 true] [17592186157384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317460 13194139534886 true] [17592186157384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317467 13194139534886 true] [17592186157385 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317474 13194139534886 true] [17592186157691 1017 #edb/ref 17592186155321 13194139534886 false] [17592186157691 1017 #edb/ref 17592186155336 13194139534886 false] [17592186157691 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317446 13194139534886 true] [17592186157691 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317449 13194139534886 true] [17592186157691 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534886 true] [17592186157691 1100 "Consider the following statement.\n\n> If $f(x) = 3e^{2x} + 2x$, then $f = O(e^{2x})$ as $x → ∞$.\n\nA proof of the statement is given below\n\n> $L1:f = O(3e^{2x} + 2x)$\n> $L2:lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{2x}{3e^{2x}}) = 0$\n> $L3:2x = O(3e^{2x})$\n> $L4:f = O(3e^{2x})$\n> $L5:f = O(e^{2x})$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\nLine $L1$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from lines\n{{field-2}} and\n{{field-3}}.\n\nLine $L5$ follows from line {{field-4}} and\n{{field-5}}." 13194139534886 false] [17592186157691 1100 "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=3{e}^{2x}+2x,$ then $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below\n\n $\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2x}{3{e}^{2x}})=0$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,2x=O(3{e}^{2x})$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x})$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O({e}^{2x})$ \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}." 13194139534886 true] [17592186157691 1145 "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{3{e}^{2x}+2x}_{⏟}}_{f})\n$$\n\nand this is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3},$ we have\n\n$$\n2x=O(3{e}^{2x}).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and the $\\text{absorption property},$ we have \n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)=O(3{e}^{2x}).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x})\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O({e}^{2x}).\n$$" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317446 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317447 13194139534886 true] [17592186317446 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317448 13194139534886 true] [17592186317446 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139534886 true] [17592186317446 1041 "field-2" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317446 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317448 13194139534886 true] [17592186317446 1110 #uuid "e4f49e08-37d3-5c47-9ff1-2d8d6d98f56b" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317447 1034 "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317447 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534886 true] [17592186317447 1111 #uuid "349f4267-da67-5757-bbdc-0ba417f2bfaa" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317448 1034 "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317448 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534886 true] [17592186317448 1111 #uuid "f0910e26-a1cc-544c-933d-8bffcf750c16" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317450 13194139534886 true] [17592186317449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317451 13194139534886 true] [17592186317449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317452 13194139534886 true] [17592186317449 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139534886 true] [17592186317449 1041 "field-4" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317449 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317450 13194139534886 true] [17592186317449 1110 #uuid "7204e117-ab7d-593f-a243-21b152e0792d" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317450 1034 "\\text{L4}" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317450 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534886 true] [17592186317450 1111 #uuid "e6e20e85-df84-572f-bdc7-d1c656459385" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317451 1034 "\\text{L2}" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317451 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534886 true] [17592186317451 1111 #uuid "c880982f-b317-5ac7-be74-1e6817e910d9" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317452 1034 "\\text{L3}" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317452 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534886 true] [17592186317452 1111 #uuid "0e73028b-f838-5a5c-a6ee-0279c4c0fb7f" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317453 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317454 13194139534886 true] [17592186317453 1089 "q-334862" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317453 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534886 true] [17592186317453 1100 "Suppose $f(x)=4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317453 1113 #uuid "495263d0-1877-5f8f-8096-f99ff3c5910b" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317453 1145 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x).\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4x+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)≈x,$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x}≈1.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(4x+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x})=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)≈x$ near $x=0,$ the quotient \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nbecomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(4+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317454 