}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317460 1113 #uuid "935514fa-c825-5eb7-b399-b6078ffdcb71" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317460 1145 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{8}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{8}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-5x+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317462 13194139534886 true] [17592186317461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317463 13194139534886 true] [17592186317461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317464 13194139534886 true] [17592186317461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317465 13194139534886 true] [17592186317461 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317466 13194139534886 true] [17592186317461 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534886 true] [17592186317461 1041 "selection" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317461 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317466 13194139534886 true] [17592186317461 1110 #uuid "30a1fc37-f60a-5996-b781-bee4bd76887c" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317462 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317462 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534886 true] [17592186317462 1111 #uuid "53b62250-3acf-5d32-95e5-f6d5b7949b88" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317463 1034 "I only" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317463 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534886 true] [17592186317463 1111 #uuid "329ece97-a84d-59cc-aaeb-648dfb55b68e" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317464 1034 "III only" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317464 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534886 true] [17592186317464 1111 #uuid "d89321e7-cd05-5d26-a5d8-72fb2f924774" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317465 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317465 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534886 true] [17592186317465 1111 #uuid "b2aa6cc5-98b0-5c89-8d13-a8dff925cdda" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317466 1034 "II only" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317466 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534886 true] [17592186317466 1111 #uuid "aaf1e78b-c38d-5f1e-bfe1-269b6c1212bc" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317467 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317468 13194139534886 true] [17592186317467 1089 "q-335019" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317467 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534886 true] [17592186317467 1100 "Suppose $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317467 1113 #uuid "258f80bc-2f49-5faa-a101-ac0575f4220f" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317467 1145 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(3{x}^{2}-x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x(3x-1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x(3x-1)}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince now direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3x-1)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{3}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534886 true] [17592186317468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317469 13194139534886 true] [17592186317468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317470 13194139534886 true] [17592186317468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317471 13194139534886 true] [17592186317468 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317472 13194139534886 true] [17592186317468 100